INT. SHUTTERED OBSERVATORY - DOME ROOM - NIGHT

A giant telescope under a retractable roof. The slit is open; a sliver of starry sky shows. Wooden floors creak underfoot. A single reading lamp glows.

Five people sit in a rough circle of folding chairs, bags at their feet.

ELSPETH COURTENAY (50s, ex-intelligence, tweed jacket) holds a silver thermos. LACHLAN MURRAY (30s, analyst, rumpled shirt) taps a laptop. OKSANA PETRENKO (40s, courier, fingerless gloves) stares at the floor. HAMZA YILMAZ (50s, broker, wool coat) picks at his cuticles. And CECILIA ROSSI (20s, junior, very new) sits on the edge of her chair like someone about to be called on.

Nobody is talking. Then:

ELSPETH
Let's be clear about why we're here.

LACHLAN
Because someone leaked.

ELSPETH
Because someone is about to.

HAMZA
You can't know that.

ELSPETH
I know what I can prove and I know what I can feel.

OKSANA
(muttering)
Feelings. Wonderful.

ELSPETH
Oksana, if you have a comment, make it to the room.

OKSANA
I said, feelings are not evidence.

ELSPETH
No. But they're how I've stayed alive.

CECILIA
Excuse me, may I ask something?

ELSPETH
You may.

CECILIA
Why are we meeting in an observatory?

LACHLAN
Nobody listens in an observatory.

HAMZA
Nobody comes to an observatory.

OKSANA
Nobody knows where it is.

ELSPETH
All three.

CECILIA
But the roof is open.

ELSPETH
It always is.

LACHLAN
(to himself)
That's actually a point.

ELSPETH
Lachlan, your laptop. Is it on the network?

LACHLAN
Offline.

ELSPETH
Prove it.

LACHLAN
Mm. Sure.

He turns the screen outward. Airplane mode, no Wi-Fi bars, no Bluetooth. She nods.

ELSPETH
Hamza. Phone.

HAMZA
In the car.

ELSPETH
Oksana.

OKSANA
Faraday bag.

ELSPETH
Cecilia.

CECILIA
I, um. I left mine at home.

ELSPETH
Good girl.

ELSPETH
Now. To business.

HAMZA
I have a question first.

LACHLAN ^
I have a question first.

ELSPETH
Make it quick.

HAMZA
If the leak is among us, how do we find it?

ELSPETH
We don't find it. We feed it.

LACHLAN
Feed it what?

ELSPETH
Something false.

OKSANA
And watch where it surfaces.

ELSPETH
Exactly.

CECILIA
But what if we're wrong?

ELSPETH
Then the false information surfaces nowhere and we have wasted a night.

HAMZA
And if we're right?

ELSPETH
Then someone in this room will be very unhappy by dawn.

A long pause. The telescope creaks overhead as the dome shifts a few degrees in the wind. A thin blade of starlight slides across Cecilia's face. She flinches.

LACHLAN
Who writes the false information?

ELSPETH
I do.

OKSANA
And who delivers it?

ELSPETH
You do. Tomorrow. To three different contacts.

OKSANA
One for each of us?

ELSPETH
Exactly one for each of you.

HAMZA
And me?

ELSPETH
You'll get a different story. A better one.

HAMZA
Why better?

ELSPETH
Because you are more careful than the others.

HAMZA
(muttering)
I don't know whether to be flattered.

ELSPETH
Don't be.

LACHLAN
Elspeth, how long until dawn?

ELSPETH
Seven hours.

CECILIA
Can we leave before dawn?

ELSPETH
No.

CECILIA
Not even to use the restroom?

ELSPETH
You may use the restroom. Take Oksana.

OKSANA
Why me?

ELSPETH
Because you're the only one who won't talk to her.

OKSANA
(to herself)
Tremendous.

She stands. Cecilia stands. The two cross the dome room, their footsteps echoing, and exit through a door at the far end.

The three remaining watch them go. The lamp flickers.

HAMZA
(very low)
She doesn't suspect, does she?

ELSPETH
Cecilia?

HAMZA
Oksana.

ELSPETH
Oksana suspects everyone. That is why I trust her.

Overhead, a single shooting star crosses the open slit of sky. Nobody sees it but Lachlan, who watches until it is gone.
